1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,480
You think you've explored medicine. In reality, you're standing in the parking lot of Disney World bragging about how fun

2
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:11,920
the pavement is. You've taken pictures of the asphalt. You've written dissertations about the parking stripes.

3
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:20,520
You've never gone inside. After a century of pharmacology, most beneficial effects of molecules on diseases remain unexplored.

4
00:00:21,140 --> 00:00:28,120
Not because biology is constrained. Because clinical trials are slow, expensive, and run by people who think "urgency" is

5
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:35,130
a type of perfume. Here's the math. It's painful but brief, like a flu shot administered by someone who hates you.

6
00:00:35,780 --> 00:00:43,960
Your Tiny Sandbox. Here is everything humanity considers "medicine" after five thousand years of civilization: Prescriptions:

7
00:00:44,660 --> 00:00:51,360
twenty thousand products approved in the U. S. Sounds impressive until you realize most are just "Tylenol, but blue."

8
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:56,480
Unique Ingredients: These products contain only one thousand six hundred fifty compounds.

9
00:00:57,360 --> 00:01:04,959
Fewer ingredients than a decent French bakery uses. Investigational Drugs: seven thousand five hundred compounds have passed

10
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:14,900
Phase One globally. Progress! Safe Stuff: The F.D.A. generally recognized as safe, or G.R.A.S., list contains six hundred

11
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:19,390
and thirty-five substances. These are things you could eat right now without dying.

12
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:27,600
Low bar. Still useful. Total unique compounds humanity knows are safe-ish: nine thousand five hundred compounds.

13
00:01:28,380 --> 00:01:31,680
That's it. That's your entire medicine cabinet after five millennia.

14
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:40,040
The Target List (Ways Your Body Breaks). The International Classification of Diseases, version ten, or I.C.D.

15
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:44,420
ten, lists fourteen thousand ways your meat can malfunction.

16
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:48,460
Humanity's most comprehensive to-do list, and you're ignoring it.

17
00:01:49,460 --> 00:01:55,440
Real Targets: After consolidation, one thousand trial-relevant diseases worth actually fixing.

18
00:01:56,460 --> 00:02:01,260
The Math You're Ignoring. Take your nine thousand five hundred safe compounds.

19
00:02:01,660 --> 00:02:08,479
Multiply by one thousand diseases. This gives you the "Combinatorial Space," which is fancy talk for "stuff you could test

20
00:02:08,479 --> 00:02:14,780
tomorrow if you weren't busy filling out forms." Nine thousand five hundred compounds multiplied by one thousand diseases

21
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:20,260
equals nine point five million plausible drug-condition combinations.

22
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:28,440
What You've Actually Tested. Approved uses: one thousand seven hundred fifty unique approved drug-disease pairings.

23
00:02:29,380 --> 00:02:37,320
Your entire medical canon. Repurposed uses: thirty percent of drugs gain at least one new indication after initial approval.

24
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:45,680
The rest never get a second date. Failed trials: Let's be generous and say you tested ten times as many things that failed.

25
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:53,500
(You did. You learned nothing from them.) Total tested: thirty-two thousand five hundred relationships.

26
00:02:54,460 --> 00:03:02,440
The Scorecard. Thirty-two thousand five hundred relationships tested divided by nine point five million possibilities equals

27
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:11,180
zero point three four two percent. Humanity has explored less than one percent of the theoretically testable drug-disease

28
00:03:11,180 --> 00:03:15,940
space. You are the detective who searched one drawer and declared the case cold.

29
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:21,060
It's like reading the first page of War and Peace and writing a book report called "It's About a Party."

30
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:27,600
Technically accurate. Cosmically insufficient. Is The Untested Stuff Useful?

31
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:34,040
You might argue, "Maybe the other 99% is useless!". Biology would like a word.

32
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:43,100
Mapping over three hundred and fifty thousand clinical trials showed that only 12% of the human interactome has ever been

33
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:48,300
targeted by drugs. You're ignoring 88% of your own biology.

34
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:53,980
It's like having a 100-room mansion and living exclusively in the bathroom.

35
00:03:54,920 --> 00:04:02,040
Drugs are messy. They hit multiple targets. F.D.A.-approved drugs often bind to things you didn't intend.

36
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:08,120
This means "side effects" are frequently "cures for something else" that nobody bothered to notice.

37
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:17,899
Sildenafil was developed for chest pain. It failed. Its "side effect" became Viagra, then got repurposed AGAIN for pulmonary

38
00:04:17,899 --> 00:04:25,140
hypertension. Thalidomide was pulled for causing birth defects; its immunomodulatory "side effects" turned out to treat

39
00:04:25,140 --> 00:04:32,140
multiple myeloma. Two drugs. Three accidental cures. Zero systematic searches.

40
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:37,920
You keep finding cures by tripping over them in hallways. Imagine if you opened a door on purpose.

41
00:04:39,320 --> 00:04:46,260
Diseases cluster on shared biological networks. Arthritis and Alzheimer's share biological pathways.

42
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:53,400
Lithium was prescribed for gout in the eighteen hundreds; decades later, it turned out to be the gold standard treatment

43
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:59,440
for bipolar disorder. Nobody connected the dots because nobody was drawing lines between diseases.

44
00:05:00,240 --> 00:05:05,400
The drug that treats your grandfather's inflammation might fix your grandmother's neurodegeneration.

45
00:05:06,180 --> 00:05:12,040
You won't find out, because testing it would require paperwork that takes longer than Grandma has left.

46
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:21,040
The Universe is Big. You Are Small. So far we only counted molecules humanity ALREADY HAS.

47
00:05:21,560 --> 00:05:25,700
The stuff in your cupboards. If you look at what's chemically possible, it gets embarrassing.

48
00:05:27,020 --> 00:05:33,920
Chemical Space is estimated to contain ten to the twenty-third to ten to the sixtieth power drug-like molecules.

49
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:38,600
That's more candidates than there are atoms in your body. Or stars in the galaxy.

50
00:05:39,380 --> 00:05:48,320
Or excuses from your regulatory agencies. Z.I.N.C. Twenty-Two contains tens of billions of purchasable virtual molecules.

51
00:05:48,820 --> 00:05:56,320
You could order them online. You haven't. You have made less than ten to the negative fortieth of possible molecules.

52
00:05:57,240 --> 00:06:04,480
Tested divided by possible equals approximately ten to the negative twentieth to ten to the negative fiftieth.

53
00:06:05,940 --> 00:06:14,860
EFFECTIVELY ZERO. You haven't even started. This is like exploring Earth by looking at one grain of sand on one beach in

54
00:06:14,860 --> 00:06:19,020
New Jersey, then declaring you've "seen the world." You haven't.

55
00:06:19,740 --> 00:06:24,920
You've seen a grain of sand. In New Jersey. Why You Are Still Sick.

56
00:06:26,040 --> 00:06:31,380
It's not because science is hard. It's because you've built a system that is allergic to answers.

57
00:06:32,120 --> 00:06:40,720
A typical Phase Two to Three R.C.T. costs thirty to one hundred million dollars (Median cost per patient forty-one thousand

58
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:45,120
dollars). At that price, you only test "sure things." Safe bets.

59
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:54,140
Boring bets. Bets that don't cure cancer. Clinical trials are a velvet-rope nightclub where the bouncer is a regulatory

60
00:06:54,140 --> 00:07:03,020
agency and the cover charge is a hundred million dollars. Herd Mentality: Trials overwhelmingly test the same few biological

61
00:07:03,020 --> 00:07:10,120
targets due to preferential attachment dynamics. Everyone studies what everyone else studies because that's how you get

62
00:07:10,120 --> 00:07:16,500
grants. It's like if every explorer only went to France because France already had good reviews on TripAdvisor.

63
00:07:17,520 --> 00:07:22,800
Meanwhile, undiscovered continents full of cures sit there, undiscovered.

64
00:07:23,960 --> 00:07:33,180
No Profit: Most molecules have no patent. No patent equals no one hundred million dollar trial which equals no approval.

65
00:07:33,240 --> 00:07:40,940
Metformin, a diabetes drug from the 1950s, shows persistent signals for reducing cancer risk and extending lifespan.

66
00:07:40,960 --> 00:07:49,920
It costs pennies. It's off-patent. The trial to prove it works for aging, Targeting Aging with Metformin, or T.A.M.E., spent

67
00:07:49,920 --> 00:07:55,820
years begging for funding because no pharmaceutical company will bankroll a drug they can't monopolize.

68
00:07:56,740 --> 00:08:02,100
The molecule that saves your life has to make money first. If it can't, it won't.

69
00:08:02,740 --> 00:08:10,800
You die. Quarterly earnings go up. Neglect: If you have a rare disease with no market, you don't get a drug.

70
00:08:11,340 --> 00:08:19,320
Your cells malfunction in an unprofitable way. The invisible hand of the market has weighed your life against quarterly

71
00:08:19,320 --> 00:08:24,050
earnings and found you wanting. Sorry about your death. It was a business decision.

72
00:08:25,540 --> 00:08:33,659
How Long to Explore Everything? At the current pace of three thousand three hundred trials per year, how long would it take

73
00:08:33,659 --> 00:08:40,480
to systematically test therapeutic possibilities? Tier 1: Single Compounds (Most Conservative).

74
00:08:41,480 --> 00:08:50,320
Testing every known safe compound against every major disease: Nine point five million combinations divided by three thousand

75
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:55,760
three hundred trials per year equals two thousand eight hundred and eighty years.

76
00:08:56,320 --> 00:09:00,940
That's almost three thousand years to explore compounds we *already know are safe*.

77
00:09:01,560 --> 00:09:04,300
By that math, we won't finish until the year five thousand.

78
00:09:05,520 --> 00:09:13,160
Tier 2: Combination Therapies (Still Defensible). Modern medicine increasingly relies on multi-drug cocktails.

79
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:20,260
Oncology, H.I.V., cardiology, psychiatry: all routinely use drug combinations.

80
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:26,480
So the real question isn't "does this one drug work?" It's "which combinations work?"

81
00:09:27,540 --> 00:09:36,020
Testing pairwise combinations of safe compounds: Forty-five point one billion combinations divided by three thousand three

82
00:09:36,020 --> 00:09:40,760
hundred trials per year equals thirteen point seven million years.

83
00:09:41,540 --> 00:09:48,360
That's longer than Homo sapiens has existed as a species. And that's using only compounds with established safety data,

84
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:52,850
testing only pairs. Not triplets. God help you if you want to try triplets.

85
00:09:54,060 --> 00:10:03,080
Tier 3: The Full Chemical Universe (Illustrative). Beyond known compounds lies the chemical space of ten to the twenty-third

86
00:10:03,080 --> 00:10:11,920
power to ten to the sixtieth power drug-like molecules. At current trial capacity, exploring even the *synthesizable* fraction

87
00:10:11,920 --> 00:10:19,120
would take longer than the universe has existed. The universe is thirteen point eight billion years old.

88
00:10:19,740 --> 00:10:24,940
You need more time than that. To test pills. Pills that already exist.

89
00:10:25,620 --> 00:10:29,630
We haven't even started. But Won't We Run Out of Easy Discoveries?

90
00:10:30,680 --> 00:10:35,740
Some will argue "diminishing returns": the more we search, the harder each new discovery becomes.

91
00:10:36,560 --> 00:10:40,840
This assumes we've already picked the low-hanging fruit. We haven't picked *any* fruit.

92
00:10:41,360 --> 00:10:43,980
We're still in the parking lot arguing about whether fruit exists.

93
00:10:44,660 --> 00:10:49,120
Diminishing returns only kicks in after you've explored a significant fraction of the space.

94
00:10:49,680 --> 00:10:56,160
At less than one percent explored, every trial teaches you something new about human biology, improving your ability to

95
00:10:56,260 --> 00:11:01,520
predict what works next. Learning compounds. Depletion is negligible.

96
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:06,580
You are nowhere near the bottom of the barrel because you have not yet located the barrel.

97
00:11:07,400 --> 00:11:14,620
The math: even with weak learning, diminishing returns only matters after exploring about thirty-seven percent of therapeutic

98
00:11:14,620 --> 00:11:20,440
space. For combination therapies, that crossover is about seven million years away at current pace.

99
00:11:21,040 --> 00:11:24,640
You cannot have diminishing returns when you've barely started looking.

100
00:11:25,280 --> 00:11:33,400
See the formal analysis for the equations. The Fix. The Oxford RECOVERY Trial (COVID-19) proved that large-scale pragmatic

101
00:11:33,400 --> 00:11:38,820
trials can run for about five hundred dollars and deliver results in less than one hundred days.

102
00:11:39,460 --> 00:11:43,880
Five hundred dollars versus forty-one thousand dollars. Read those numbers again.

103
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:51,360
One of them is the reason people are dying. With a decentralized framework for drug assessment, or D.F.D.A.

104
00:11:51,420 --> 00:11:59,860
(a decentralized F.D.A.), trial capacity increases by twelve point three times: Nine point five million combinations divided

105
00:11:59,860 --> 00:12:05,540
by forty thousand seven hundred trials per year equals two hundred thirty-four years.

106
00:12:06,200 --> 00:12:09,840
Still a long time, but at least it's a number with fewer digits than a phone book.

107
00:12:10,420 --> 00:12:16,640
And the first few decades would capture the highest-value discoveries (the low-hanging fruit that current trial scarcity

108
00:12:16,960 --> 00:12:19,960
forces you to ignore while people die in the waiting room).

109
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:28,740
More importantly, D.F.D.A. (a decentralized F.D.A.) builds *permanent infrastructure* for systematic therapeutic exploration.

110
00:12:29,380 --> 00:12:35,880
The goal isn't to "finish." It's to stop leaving cures on the table while you form subcommittees to discuss whether to form

111
00:12:35,880 --> 00:12:39,740
committees to review the proposal to consider them. The Bottom Line.

112
00:12:40,420 --> 00:12:47,880
Humanity has empirically tested less than one percent of the drug-disease relationships that can be tested using existing

113
00:12:47,900 --> 00:12:56,140
safe compounds. If you include the larger chemical universe, humanity has explored roughly one-quadrillionth to one-quintillionth

114
00:12:56,200 --> 00:13:01,100
of what is possible (ten to the power of negative twelve to ten to the power of negative fifty).

115
00:13:02,080 --> 00:13:05,860
There are numbers so small that mathematicians get depressed looking at them.

116
00:13:06,640 --> 00:13:11,980
These are those numbers. The overwhelming majority of therapeutic potential remains untouched.

117
00:13:12,560 --> 00:13:17,280
Not because cures are impossible. Because trials are too expensive and too slow.

118
00:13:18,280 --> 00:13:25,860
A decentralized, automated pragmatic trial system is the only route to systematically explore the remaining ninety-nine

119
00:13:25,860 --> 00:13:33,060
point nine nine nine percent of medical space. You're not in the parking lot because there's nothing inside Disney World.

120
00:13:33,060 --> 00:13:38,820
You're in the parking lot because the entrance fee is one hundred million dollars and requires seventeen years of paperwork.

121
00:13:39,460 --> 00:13:43,000
People are dying in the parking lot. The rides are right there.

122
00:13:43,060 --> 00:13:46,520
You can hear the screaming. (Fun screaming. Cure screaming.).

123
00:13:47,160 --> 00:13:50,080
The cures are inside. Open the gate.
